            <ArticleTitle>Laparoscopic plication and suspension of the round ligament for chronic pelvic pain and dyspareunia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>547-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe a technique of uterine suspension using round ligaments to relieve pain in selected patients with various degrees of uterine retroversion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective clinical study (Canadian Task Force classification II-2).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University-based center for reproductive medicine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty women who underwent laparoscopy for investigation of chronic pelvic pain (CPP) and dyspareunia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Round ligaments were plicated using a modification of intracorporeal knot tying during laparoscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The extent of plication was planned to elevate the uterus and bring it minimally forward. Mean +/- SD operating time was 14+/-4 minutes. Pain scores before and after surgery were 4.5+/-1.0 and 1.6+/-0.6, respectively (p&lt;0.001). There were no complications during or after surgery. Only three women had minimal dyspareunia postoperatively, although they had anteverted uteri. One patient had severe dyspareunia that developed 7 months after surgery and continued through the second year of follow-up. Nineteen women with anteverted uteri were free of dyspareunia after 2 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Round ligament plication is safe and effective in patients with retroverted uteri and dyspareunia or CPP.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
